## Interface: 20506
## Category: Commander
## Title: Commander Arena Frames
## Author: Devin
## Version: 1.8.0
## Notes: Enemy frames built for arena, and the other half of Commander Party Frames — that board is your team, this one is theirs. Health and live resource per enemy (a healer's mana is the number an arena is most often decided by and the one thing they cannot hide, so the power strip is on, tall enough to read, and carries a percentage of its own), a real cast bar per enemy to time a kick against with channels drawn draining rather than filling, their PvP trinket read from the client itself rather than guessed — so it is right from the first second of the game instead of from the first time you watch them press it — every cooldown they spend learned from the combat log against a book of 120 abilities whose ids, cooldowns and durations all come from the client's own tables, and -- the thing a board is asked about more than anything else -- WHO CANNOT ACT: a bar across the enemy's own health bar naming the control on them, SAPPED, FEARED, STUNNED, TRAPPED, BLINDED, SILENCED, with the seconds left on it, a fill that drains, and what the NEXT one in that category would land at, so the question "is there a re-stun on the other side of this" is answered on the same line as the clock. Silences are in it and in nothing else on the board, because TBC gives silence no diminishing returns at all and so a locked-out healer carries no bracket for anything else to draw. The same strip carries the other four states that must stop a cast rather than be noticed -- an immunity, a lockout, a drink, a stealth -- and it carries the health percentage with it, because a bar that covers the health bar and takes the number away has removed the one figure an arena is most often decided by. The headline is diminishing returns: a bracket per category per enemy with TBC's own rules, generated from the client's spell data through the classifier the server itself uses — so Kidney Shot is on its own ladder (a rogue can Cheap Shot into a full Kidney), proc stuns ladder separately from cast ones while Charge and Intercept do not, Freezing Trap is not the Polymorph ladder, and silence has no bracket at all because silence DR does not exist until 3.0.8. Two things make it correct rather than approximately correct: the fifteen-second reset clock starts when the aura FADES rather than when it lands and freezes entirely while anything in the category is still up, and CC broken by damage is caught — it arrives as SPELL_AURA_BROKEN instead of SPELL_AURA_REMOVED, and a tracker that misses it leaves every broken stun's bracket stuck for the rest of the game. An ability never seen used is drawn as UNKNOWN rather than ready, and a specialization is shown only once the enemy has PROVEN it by casting something nothing else can cast — the board never guesses a spec from a class. The board sizes itself to the bracket: identity sits ON the health bar rather than above it, the cooldown strip shrinks its icons just enough to land a whole class book on one line instead of wrapping into a block taller than the enemy's own health bar, and from 4v4 up every vertical dimension scales down and the spacing halves -- so a 5v5 board is a little over half the height it would otherwise be, with the health bar itself nearly three times taller. The look is the one every arena frame since Gladius has trained the eye on, done with the suite's own art: a black line around the class square, the bars, the trinket and the cast bar; a dark plate under each row so its cast bar, cooldowns and auras read as ONE enemy; class-coloured bars; a clock face swept over every icon that is counting; a spark on the cast; the trinket at the row's height beside the class square; a white pulse on the rim the moment a cooldown or the trinket comes back; and a thin bar for the enemy's Felhunter, Succubus or Water Elemental with its family and health, because killing the Felhunter is a decision an arena is made of. Three size presets (Compact, Standard, Gladdy-sized) move every slider at once. Ninety settings, a three-enemy test board that runs the real painters over fabricated state so it can be placed anywhere without queueing, and optional secure click-cast rows carrying the full modifier x mouse-button matrix — bindable to targeting, focus, assist, or any spell this character has actually trained, with a red rim on any cell bound to something you cannot cast, because that is a click which saves perfectly and then does nothing in a game.
